  "title": "Why is the Intermediate Value Theorem a no-jump theorem?",
  "body": " Why is the Intermediate Value Theorem a no-jump theorem?   A function that jumps from to can miss the intermediate value even on a closed interval. In your own words, why is continuity not decoration, and what illegal move would it be to apply the theorem to a graph with a break?    The theorem’s machine needs a graph that cannot skip heights. If a hypothesis is missing, the output is no longer guaranteed.   "
