  "id": "sec-5-5-derivatives-of-trigonometric-functions",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-5-5-derivatives-of-trigonometric-functions.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "5.5",
  "title": "Derivatives of trigonometric functions",
  "body": " Derivatives of trigonometric functions  The chain rule let us follow change through layers. Implicit differentiation let us find slopes on curves that were not solved for . But many motions are not built from powers and products. They repeat.  A wheel turns. A pendulum swings. A spring moves down, then up, then down again. The functions that describe repeating motion are sine and cosine.  The surprise is that their derivatives are not new functions. They are sine and cosine again, shifted and signed.   Radians and the limit  Before differentiating sine, we need to be careful about angle measure.  On the unit circle, an angle of radians cuts off an arc of length . That is why radians are the natural angle unit for calculus. A small angle is also a small distance traveled along the circle.  Degrees do not have this property. A degree is a human subdivision of a full turn. A radian is built from length.   For , the small triangle lies inside the sector, which lies inside the larger triangle.      For , compare three areas in Figure :   These areas are   So   Multiplying by ,   Since , we have   Divide by the positive number :   Taking reciprocals reverses the inequalities:   As ,   Therefore, by the Squeeze Theorem,   The function is even, because   Thus the left-hand limit is the same as the right-hand limit. Therefore   This limit is the small-angle fact behind the derivative of sine. For very small , measured in radians,   On the unit circle, the vertical rise and the arc length are almost the same when the angle is tiny.  We will also need one companion limit:   Use the identity   Then   As ,   and   So    Why must the small-angle limit use radians?   On the unit circle, an angle of radians cuts off an arc of length , and the squeeze argument gives . In your own words, why are radians the natural unit for this limit, and why is a statement about tiny rise versus tiny arc length?    A radian is built from length. A degree is a human subdivision of a full turn and does not make small angle equal small arc.      The derivative of sine  Let   We compute the derivative from the definition:   Use the addition formula   Then   Group the terms:   Now take the limit:   Thus   This formula has a simple geometric meaning. At , the sine curve is rising with slope , and   At , the sine curve has a high point, so its slope is , and   At , the sine curve is falling with slope , and    The derivative of the sine curve is the cosine curve.       Why is the derivative of sine equal to cosine?   After the addition formula splits the difference quotient, the two companion limits produce . In your own words, why does that formula match the sine graph at , at , and at ?    Compare the slope of the sine curve at those three inputs with the corresponding cosine values. Rising, level, and falling should appear in order.     Example   Differentiate   Use the product rule:   So      Example   Differentiate   The outside function is sine. The inside function is   By the chain rule,   Thus       The derivative of cosine  Cosine is a shifted sine:   Differentiate using the chain rule:   The derivative of sine is cosine, so   But   Therefore   The minus sign matters. At , cosine has a high point, so the slope is . At small positive , cosine is decreasing, so the slope is negative. The formula   records exactly that.   Why does the derivative of cosine carry a minus sign?   Cosine is a shifted sine, and the chain rule then produces . In your own words, why is the minus sign required by the graph at the right of , rather than an extra decoration?    At , cosine has a high point. Immediately afterward the graph is decreasing, so the slope must be negative.     Example   Differentiate   Let   Then   By the chain rule,   Since   we get      Example   Differentiate   Using the product rule,   Thus   Using the identity   we can also write   This is a useful reminder: different-looking derivative answers can be the same after a trigonometric identity.      Tangent, secant, cosecant, and cotangent  The remaining trigonometric derivatives follow from sine and cosine together with the quotient and reciprocal rules.  Start with tangent:   By the quotient rule,   So   Since   we get   Now use reciprocal forms.  Since   the reciprocal rule gives   Rewrite:   Thus   Similarly,   Therefore   So   Finally,   Using the quotient rule,   Thus   We collect the six formulas:   The negative signs belong to the cofunctions:   That is a useful memory aid, but the formulas should also make sense from the graphs.   Example   Differentiate   Use the quotient rule:   Expand the numerator:   Since   the numerator becomes   Therefore   So   This derivative exists wherever the original function exists, so we exclude points where   That happens when   or       Why radians are required  The formulas   are true when is measured in radians.  If is measured in degrees, then the function   really means   By the chain rule,   So   The extra factor appears because one degree is only radians.   Why do the standard trig formulas assume radians?   If the input is in degrees, the chain rule inserts an extra factor of . In your own words, why is that factor not a later convention, and why do sine and cosine still matter for repeating motion even after the formulas are in place?    One degree is only radians. Their derivatives cycle back to sine and cosine, which is why they fit springs and wheels.     Warning  The standard trigonometric derivative formulas assume radian measure. Radians are not a convention added later. They are the unit in which small angle and small arc length agree.     A preview of simple harmonic motion  A spring gives a concrete reason why sine and cosine derivatives matter.  Suppose a mass hangs from a spring. Pull it down centimeters from its resting position and release it. A simple model for its position is   where is measured in seconds and is measured in centimeters from the rest position. Positive means downward.  The velocity is   Using the chain rule,   So   The acceleration is   Differentiate again:   Thus   But   so   Therefore   The acceleration points opposite the displacement. When the mass is below its rest position, the acceleration points upward. When it is above its rest position, the acceleration points downward. The spring pulls back toward equilibrium.   For simple harmonic motion, acceleration is proportional to displacement and points in the opposite direction.      This is the first glimpse of a differential equation:   It says the law of motion in terms of derivatives. Later we will study such equations directly. For now, the important point is that sine and cosine are built for repeated motion because their derivatives cycle back to sine and cosine.  Trigonometric functions describe quantities that repeat. Another family describes quantities that grow or decay by multiplying: populations, radioactive substances, and continuously compounded money. Their derivatives lead to the number .   "
