  "type": "Section",
  "number": "6.4",
  "title": "Concavity and second derivatives",
  "body": " Concavity and second derivatives  The first derivative tells whether a function is rising or falling. It does not tell the whole shape.  A car can move forward while speeding up. It can also move forward while slowing down. In both cases the velocity is positive, but the motion feels different.  Graphs have the same distinction. A graph can be increasing while bending upward, or increasing while bending downward. The second derivative measures that bending.   Velocity changing: acceleration  Let   Then   and   The velocity is increasing. The object is speeding up. The graph of bends upward.  Now let   on . Then   This velocity is positive on , so the position is still increasing. But the velocity is decreasing, because   The object is moving forward while slowing down. The graph bends downward.   Both positions increase on the interval, but one bends upward and the other bends downward.       Second derivative  If is differentiable, then the derivative of is called the second derivative of :   In motion, if is position, then   is velocity, and   is acceleration.   The units also tell the story. If is measured in meters and in seconds, then   has units   and   has units   Acceleration measures how fast velocity changes.   Why can two increasing graphs still have different shapes?   A car can move forward while speeding up, or move forward while slowing down. In both cases velocity is positive. In your own words, why does the first derivative miss this distinction, and what extra question does the second derivative ask?     tells whether the graph is rising or falling. tells whether those slopes themselves are increasing or decreasing.      Concave up and concave down  The word concavity describes the direction a graph bends.  If the slopes are increasing as we move left to right, the graph is concave up . It bends like a cup.  If the slopes are decreasing as we move left to right, the graph is concave down . It bends like a cap.   Concavity  Suppose is differentiable on an interval .  The function is concave up on if is increasing on .  The function is concave down on if is decreasing on .   This definition says that concavity is really a statement about how the tangent slopes change.    Because the second derivative is the derivative of , the first derivative sign rule applied to gives the main test.   Second derivative sign rule  Suppose exists on an interval .  If   on , then is concave up on .  If   on , then is concave down on .   The reason is direct. If , then is increasing. If , then is decreasing.   Concave up means slopes increase. Concave down means slopes decrease.       Why is concavity a statement about changing slopes?   Concave up means is increasing; concave down means is decreasing. In your own words, why is that not the same as being positive, and why does the sign of become a test for concavity?    Apply the first derivative sign rule to itself. A cup-shaped graph is one whose tangent slopes are getting steeper as you move right.      A sign chart for the second derivative  Consider again   Then   and   The second derivative is negative when and positive when .   The graph bends downward to the left of , and upward to the right of .   The sign of gives the concavity of .         This example also shows a new kind of point: the graph changes concavity at .    Inflection points  A point where the concavity changes is called an inflection point.   Inflection point  A point on the graph of is an inflection point if the concavity of changes at .   The number is often found by looking for places where   or where   does not exist. But these are only candidates. The concavity must actually change.   Why is only a candidate for an inflection point?   An inflection point is a point where concavity actually changes. In your own words, why might we look where is zero or undefined, and why can fail to produce an inflection, as with at the origin?    This is the second-derivative version of the warning about critical numbers. A zero second derivative may or may not be a place where the bending switches.     an inflection point   For   we have   The second derivative changes from negative to positive at . Therefore is an inflection point.     a false candidate   For   we have   At ,   But   for . The graph is concave up on both sides of . So is not an inflection point.   This is the second-derivative version of the warning about critical numbers. A zero derivative may or may not give an extremum. A zero second derivative may or may not give an inflection point.      The second derivative test  The first derivative test studies the sign of on both sides of a critical number. The second derivative test is faster when it works.  The idea is simple. Suppose   If   then near , the derivative is increasing. Since , the derivative tends to be negative just before and positive just after . That means decreases into and increases away from , so gives a local minimum.  If   then is decreasing. The derivative tends to be positive before and negative after , so gives a local maximum.   Second derivative test  Suppose , and suppose is continuous near .  If   then has a local minimum at .  If   then has a local maximum at .  If   the test gives no conclusion.    Proof  Assume first that   Since is continuous near , it stays positive on some small interval around . On that interval, is increasing.  Because   we have   for close to , and   for close to . By the first derivative test, has a local minimum at .  The case   is similar. Then is decreasing near , so changes from positive to negative at . By the first derivative test, has a local maximum at .    Why can the second derivative test succeed, and why can it stay silent?   If and , then is a local minimum. In your own words, why does a positive second derivative at a critical number force the first derivative to change from negative to positive, and why does give no conclusion?    Near , means is increasing through zero. When , the same first-derivative sign pattern can be a min, a max, or neither.     Example   Let   The critical numbers come from   So   are critical numbers.  The second derivative is   At ,   so has a local maximum at . The value is   At ,   so has a local minimum at . The value is   This agrees with the first derivative sign chart, but it is quicker once is known.      When the second derivative test is inconclusive  The last line of the test matters:   Three different things can happen.   A local minimum  Let   Then   At ,   The second derivative test gives no conclusion. But for all , so has a local minimum at .    A local maximum  Let   Then   At ,   Again the test gives no conclusion. But for all , so has a local maximum at .    No extremum  Let   Then   At ,   But is increasing through , so there is no local maximum or local minimum there.   It means the test is silent. We must return to the first derivative test, the shape of the graph, or another argument.     Where this points  The first derivative tells where the graph rises and falls. The second derivative tells where the graph bends upward and downward.  Together, they give a much richer picture:    But a graph can also have asymptotes, holes, intercepts, and end behavior. The next step is to put all the evidence together into a graph-sketching method that is guided by calculus rather than by guesswork.   "
