  "id": "sec-7-3-linear-approximation-in-applications",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-7-3-linear-approximation-in-applications.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "7.3",
  "title": "Linear approximation in applications",
  "body": " Linear approximation in applications  Optimization asked for the best choice. Related rates asked how fast quantities change together.  Measurements ask a quieter question.   A radius may be measured only to the nearest millimeter. An angle may be off by one degree. A price model may be only an approximation. Calculus gives a first answer through the tangent line.  Recall the local linear approximation:   The derivative is the multiplier that turns a small input change into a small output change.   That is the practical meaning of the derivative in this section.   Estimating hard numbers quickly  Some numbers are hard to compute exactly, but close to numbers we know well.  For example, is close to . Since   we can use the tangent line to   at   The derivative is   So   The linear approximation at is   Thus   For ,   So   This is close. In fact,   so is slightly larger than .   Near , the tangent line to gives a quick estimate of .      The general name for this tangent-line function is the linearization .   Linearization  If is differentiable at , then the linearization of at is   For close to ,    The word “close” matters. A tangent line is a local model. It is meant for nearby inputs.   Example   Use linear approximation to estimate   Let   The number is close to , and   So take   The derivative is   At ,   Since   we have   Therefore   The linearization is   For ,      A warning about going too far  Linear approximation is local. For   the linearization at is   At ,   while   That is useful.  At ,   but   That is not useful. The tangent line knew the function near , not near the vertical asymptote at .   Local means local  A tangent line is a good model only while the graph has not had time to bend much.     Why does local mean the tangent line is only a nearby model?   A linearization can estimate a nearby hard number quickly, but going too far from the known point makes the estimate worse. In your own words, why does the quality of the approximation depend on remaining close to the expansion point?    A tangent line is a good model only while the graph has not had time to bend much.      Error propagation  Suppose an input is measured with a small error. Write the measured change as   If   then the exact output change is   The derivative gives the approximation   This is often written using differentials:   When we use the differential to estimate an actual small change, we set   and then use    error in the volume of a sphere   A spherical ball has radius measured as   with possible measurement error   Estimate the possible error in the computed volume.  The volume of a sphere is   Differentiate:   At   we get   Since   the maximum estimated volume error is   So   The symbol means “approximately less than or equal to.” This is an estimated error bound, not an exact one.   For a sphere, : surface area times a small change in radius.      This formula also has a geometric meaning. Increasing the radius by a small amount adds a thin shell. Its volume is approximately   That is exactly the differential .     Absolute error form  If has maximum possible error   then the output error is estimated by   So a practical estimate is   The derivative acts as an error multiplier.    Why is the derivative the multiplier of a small input change?   Local linear approximation says . In your own words, why does that make a converter from a small input error into a small output error, and why is this the practical meaning of the derivative in measurement?    The tangent line is the first model of how output responds to input. Differentials are the same idea written as .      Relative and percentage error  Absolute error tells the size of the error in the units of the quantity. Relative error compares the error to the size of the quantity itself.  If , then   The percentage error is   For estimates, we use differentials:    error in the area of a disk   A circular disk has measured radius   with possible measurement error   The area is   So   At ,   Thus the maximum estimated area error is   Now compute relative error:   Substitute the values:   As a percentage,   So the radius error of about   produces an area error of about   The percentage error doubled because area depends on the square of radius.     Power laws  Suppose   where and are constants. Then   Divide by    So   For a power law, the relative output error is approximately times the relative input error.   This is why small measurement errors can grow when a formula contains powers.    Why is relative error a different question from absolute error?   Absolute error keeps the units of the quantity. Relative error compares the error to the size of the quantity itself. In your own words, why can a small absolute error still be a large relative error, or the reverse, and why do power laws make relative error especially readable?    An error of one centimeter is huge for a bacterium and tiny for a planet. For , relative error in is about times relative error in .      Sensitivity in measurement  The derivative measures sensitivity.  If   then   has units   A large value of means that a small input error can create a large output error. A small value of means the output is less sensitive to input error.   Why does a large mean high sensitivity?   The derivative has units of output per input. In your own words, why does a steep graph turn a small measurement error into a large output error, and why might a surveyor’s angle error matter more at some elevations than at others?    Sensitivity is . Where the function is steep, the output is more easily disturbed by input error.     measuring height using an angle   A surveyor stands   from the base of a tower. The angle of elevation to the top is . The height of the tower is modeled by   Differentiate:   The derivative formula for assumes that is measured in radians. A one-degree error is   radians.  If   then   So a one-degree measurement error gives   If   then   The same one-degree error gives   The distance error is the same. The angle error is the same. The sensitivity is not the same. Near a vertical angle, the tangent function changes very quickly.   The model becomes very sensitive when is large.       Sensitivity  For , the size of tells how strongly errors in are amplified into errors in .   Linear approximation gives us three related calculations:   So far we have mostly used the first and third. The second one is about to become a method for solving equations.  If is not zero, how far should we move so that becomes zero? The tangent line gives the first guess. Repeating that guess gives Newton’s method.     "
