  "id": "sec-7-5-exponential-growth-and-decay",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-7-5-exponential-growth-and-decay.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "7.5",
  "title": "Exponential growth and decay",
  "body": " Exponential growth and decay  Newton’s method used a tangent line to solve an equation. Linear approximation helped us decide how far to move.  But many models do not first give an equation like   They give a law about change.  A population grows because each individual helps create more individuals. A radioactive substance decays because each atom has a chance to decay. A bank account grows because interest is earned on the current balance. In each case, the rate of change is tied to the amount already present.  That leads to the simplest and most important differential equation:    The differential equation  A differential equation is an equation involving an unknown function and one or more of its derivatives.  For example,   does not ask for a number . It asks for a function whose derivative is always times the function itself.  The constant controls the model.   The units of are important. If is measured in grams and in years, then   has units   So in   the constant must have units   It is a relative rate of change.   a first growth model   Suppose a small bacterial culture satisfies   where is measured in hours. If   find .  The equation says that the population grows at a rate equal to of its current size per hour. We need a function whose derivative is times the function.  Since   a natural solution is   Use the initial condition:   Since ,   Therefore   The population grows exponentially.   The equation gives exponential growth when and exponential decay when .      The example gives the general pattern.   Exponential growth and decay equation  If   and   then       Proof  Suppose satisfies   Multiply by . Let   Then, by the product rule,   Factor:   Since , we get   So is constant. Therefore   and hence   Using ,   So   This proves the formula.   Meaning of the equation   is equivalent to   when . The relative rate of change is constant. That is the defining feature of exponential growth and decay.     Why does ask for a function rather than a number?   A differential equation involves an unknown function and its derivative. In your own words, why is a law about change rather than a formula for , and why does the sign of decide growth versus decay?    The equation says the rate is always proportional to the amount present. The solutions are the functions that obey that law.      Half-life and doubling time  The constant is not always the most natural way to describe exponential change.  For growth, we often describe a quantity by its doubling time : the time it takes to multiply by .  For decay, we often describe a quantity by its half-life : the time it takes to multiply by .  Suppose   If , the doubling time satisfies   So   Assuming ,   Take natural logarithms:   Therefore   For decay, . The half-life satisfies   So   Thus   Taking logarithms,   Therefore   when .   Why do exponential models have a constant doubling time or half-life?   If , the time needed to double (when ) or to halve (when ) does not depend on the starting amount. In your own words, why is that a consequence of constant relative growth, rather than an extra assumption?    Multiplying the current amount by a fixed factor is the same as adding a fixed amount of time, because the exponential turns addition in time into multiplication of size.     decay from a half-life   A substance has half-life hours. If the initial amount is , find the amount after hours.  Since hours is   half-lives, the amount is multiplied by four times:   Thus   Using the exponential formula, half-life gives   So   This can also be written as   That form makes the half-life visible.   Half-life form  If a quantity has half-life , then     Doubling-time form  If a quantity has doubling time , then    These are the same exponential model written in a way that matches the information given.     estimating a doubling time   A population grows according to   where is measured in years. Its doubling time is   Since   we get   The population doubles in about years.      Continuously compounded interest  Money in an account can also be modeled by   Here   is the account balance after years, and is the annual interest rate written as a decimal.  If   then the account grows at per year. The continuously compounded model is   So   More generally, if the initial deposit is , then    Example   Suppose dollars is invested at annual interest, compounded continuously. Find the value after years.  The model is   Thus   Since   we get   dollars.   Interest as a rate law  Continuous compounding means that interest is earned on the current balance at every instant:   That rate law forces the exponential formula    There is a useful connection with ordinary compounding. If interest is compounded times per year, then   As gets larger, this approaches   So continuous compounding is the limiting case of more and more frequent compounding.      Newton’s law of cooling  Not every exponential model has the form   A hot cup of coffee in a room does not cool toward . It cools toward the room temperature.  The natural changing quantity is not the temperature itself, but the temperature difference between the coffee and the room.  Let   and let   Newton’s law of cooling says:   The difference   decays exponentially.  Let   Since is constant,   The cooling law becomes   Therefore   Since   we get    Why are interest and cooling the same kind of law?   Continuously compounded interest and Newton’s law of cooling look like different stories. In your own words, why are they both versions of a rate proportional to the current amount (or to the current difference from equilibrium), and what does that common structure buy us?    Once the rate law is or , the same exponential solution applies. The application changes; the differential equation does not.     cooling coffee   A cup of coffee is when placed in a room whose temperature is   After minutes, the coffee is   Assuming Newton’s law of cooling, estimate the temperature after minutes.  The model is   So   Use :   Then   so   Thus   Now find . Since   we have   Therefore   Compute:   So   Thus    Newton’s law of cooling says the temperature difference decays exponentially.          What exponential models assume  Exponential models are powerful because they are simple. Their simplicity is also their danger.  The equation   says that the relative rate of change is constant:   If that ratio is not constant, the model is not exponential.   Warning example  Let   Then   The quantity is increasing for , but it is not growing exponentially. Its relative rate of change is   This depends on . It is not constant.  So increasing does not mean exponential. Even fast increasing does not mean exponential. Exponential growth means constant relative growth.   How to recognize the exponential model  A quantity is modeled by   when its rate of change is proportional to its current amount.  Equivalently, when ,   is constant.   Exponential growth is often a good first approximation. But it cannot continue forever in a world with limited space, food, money, or people. The next model keeps the early exponential behavior but builds in a ceiling.    Why must the relative rate be constant for a model to be exponential?   The equation says . A quantity can increase without being exponential. In your own words, why is a changing relative rate a warning that the exponential model is the wrong promise, even if the graph is rising?    Exponential models are powerful because they are simple. Their simplicity is also their danger: if is not constant, the model is not exponential.     "
