  "id": "sec-10-1-area-between-curves",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-10-1-area-between-curves.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "10.1",
  "title": "Area between curves",
  "body": " Area between curves  Area under a graph measures the region between the graph and the -axis. But the -axis is only one possible lower boundary.  Often a region lies between two curves. Then the vertical thickness of a small strip is not “height above the axis.” It is   That is the first new small piece.   Top minus bottom  Suppose two continuous functions and satisfy   on the interval . The region between their graphs is made of thin vertical strips.  At a particular , the top of the strip has height , and the bottom has height . So the strip height is   If the strip has small width , then its area is approximately   Adding the strips and taking the limit gives the area.   Area between two curves, vertical slices  If on , then the area between the graphs of and from to is    In words:   The order matters. Area is positive. The height of a vertical strip is top minus bottom, not bottom minus top.   Why is the small piece top minus bottom?   Area under one graph used height above the axis. Area between two graphs uses . In your own words, why is the thickness of a vertical strip the difference of the two boundary values, and why does the order of subtraction matter?    Ordinary area is positive. The height of a strip is the upper boundary minus the lower boundary, not the other way around.     a line over a parabola   Find the area enclosed by   and   First find the intersection points:   So   and therefore   The curves meet at   On , the line is above the parabola. For example, at ,   Thus   The area is   Compute:    A vertical strip has height , so the area is .      The integral did not measure area under one graph. It measured accumulated strip heights between two graphs.      Why we cannot skip the picture  The formula   requires knowing which graph is on top. That may change.  Consider the curves   and   They intersect when   So   or   The intersection points occur at   On the interval , the cubic is above the line. For example, at ,   so   On the interval , the line is above the cubic. For example, at ,   so   Therefore the area is   Compute the two equal parts:   Thus   If we had written   we would have gotten   because the positive and negative signed areas cancel. That is not the area of the region.   Warning  Area between curves is not found by blindly integrating one function minus the other. The integrand must be   If the top curve changes, split the integral.   This is the same lesson we learned with signed area. A definite integral may cancel positive and negative contributions. Ordinary area does not cancel.   Why can you not skip the picture?   The curves and switch which graph is on top at an intersection. In your own words, why does integrating a single difference over the whole interval fail, and why are intersections places where the description of the region may change?    A definite integral cancels negative contributions. Ordinary area does not. Split wherever top and bottom trade places.      Right minus left  Vertical slices are not always the easiest choice. Sometimes a horizontal strip has a simpler length.  For a horizontal strip, the small thickness is . Its length is   So the small area is    Area between curves, horizontal slices  If on , then the area between the curves and is    In words:    When should a strip run horizontally?   Horizontal slices accumulate . In your own words, why is the slicing direction a choice about a simple length rather than a law of nature, and what makes one direction easier than the other?    Choose the direction in which a typical strip has one formula for its length from start to finish, without needing to split the region.     two sideways parabolas   Find the area enclosed by   and   The curves meet when   Thus   so   For , the right boundary is   and the left boundary is   Therefore the area is   So   Compute:   At , this gives   At , it gives   So    A horizontal strip has length , so we integrate with respect to .      A vertical slice would have forced us to split the region into several pieces. A horizontal slice sees the whole region at once.      Splitting at intersections  Intersections are not just endpoints. They are places where the description of the region may change.  Here is a safe procedure.   Setting up an area between curves    Draw or imagine the region.    Find the intersection points.    Decide whether vertical or horizontal slices are simpler.    Write the height or length of a typical slice.    Split the integral wherever the top\/bottom or right\/left description changes.     The picture is not decoration. It tells which subtraction is legal.   one region, two vertical descriptions   Find the area of the region bounded by   and   First find intersections:   So   and therefore   The curves meet at   On the interval , test :   Thus the line is on top and the parabola is on the bottom. The area is   Compute:   At ,   At ,   So   In this example the top curve did not change. But we still found the intersections first because they gave the interval of integration.      Area from data and graphs  A formula is not always available. If a graph or table gives the vertical distance between two boundaries, the same idea still works.  Suppose a surveyor measures the distance between two shorelines at equally spaced values of . The distances are:             vertical distance          The spacing is   Using the trapezoid rule, the area is approximately   So the area is approximately   square units.  The same calculation can be written as an approximate integral:   The data gave the height difference directly. If the data instead gave separate top and bottom values, we would subtract first and then estimate the integral.   Data version  When the boundary is measured rather than given by formulas, the integral is still built from the same small piece:   A table changes how we estimate the integral, not what the integral means.   Area between curves is still area. We have only replaced a rectangle under one curve by a strip between two curves. The next step changes dimension. A strip becomes a slice.   What stays the same when the boundary is measured rather than given by a formula?   A table of shoreline distances still leads to an approximate integral of strip heights. In your own words, why does missing a formula change how we estimate the integral, but not what the integral means?    The small piece is still measured strip height times strip width. Data only change the method of adding those pieces.     "
