  "id": "sec-10-2-volumes-by-slicing",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-10-2-volumes-by-slicing.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "10.2",
  "title": "Volumes by slicing",
  "body": " Volumes by slicing  Area is two-dimensional accumulation. Volume is three-dimensional accumulation.  The idea is familiar from a loaf of bread. If we know the area of each slice and the thickness of the slices, then the volume is approximately   added over all slices.  As the slices become thinner, the approximation becomes an integral.   Cross-sectional area as a function  Suppose a solid stretches from to . At each , cut the solid by a plane perpendicular to the -axis. Let   be the area of that cross section.  A thin slice near with thickness has volume approximately   Add the slices:   Taking the limit gives the volume.   Volume by slicing  If a solid lies between and , and if the cross-sectional area perpendicular to the -axis is , then    The unit check is useful. If is measured in square centimeters and is measured in centimeters, then   has units   That is volume.   Volume by slicing: a differential slice of thickness has volume approximately .      This formula is simple, but it hides the real work. The hard part is finding .   Why is volume the integral of cross-sectional area?   A thin slice near has volume about . In your own words, why does knowing the area of each cross section, together with the thickness of the slices, determine the volume, and why is the base of the solid not enough by itself?    Volume is three-dimensional accumulation. You need both where the slices run and the area of one slice as a function of position.     a cone from its cross sections   A right circular cone has height and base radius . Put the vertex at and the base at . At position , the cross section perpendicular to the -axis is a disk.  The radius grows linearly from to . By similar triangles,   So the cross-sectional area is   The volume is   Compute:   This is the familiar cone formula. The integral explains the factor : the cross sections grow like , and       Solids of revolution: disks and washers  A common way to make a solid is to rotate a plane region around an axis. The resulting solid is called a solid of revolution .  If a vertical slice rotates around the -axis, it sweeps out a disk or a washer.   Disk slices  Suppose on . Rotate the region under   above the -axis around the -axis.  At position , the cross section perpendicular to the -axis is a disk with radius   So   Therefore     Why does rotating a radius produce a disk of area ?   If the region under rotates around the -axis, a vertical slice sweeps out a disk of radius . In your own words, why is the small volume rather than a rectangle of height ?    After rotation, the cross section perpendicular to the axis is a filled circle. Its area comes from the radius of that circle, not from the original strip’s area in the plane.     rotating   Rotate the region under   from to around the -axis.  A cross section at is a disk with radius   So   Thus    Rotating the region under around the -axis gives disk slices.         Washer slices  If a region has a hole after rotation, the cross sections are washers.  Suppose . Rotate the region between   and   around the -axis. The outer radius is . The inner radius is . The cross-sectional area is   Thus   This is the washer method.   Warning  The area of a washer is   not   For example, if and , the correct washer area is   while   The radius difference is not the area difference.     rotating a region between two curves   Rotate the region between   and   from to around the -axis.  On ,   So the outer radius is   and the inner radius is   The volume is   Compute:   The integrand is not . That would be the height of the two-dimensional region. For volume, each slice is a washer, so we need the difference of squared radii:       Non-circular cross sections  The slicing formula   does not require circular slices. Any cross-sectional shape is allowed, as long as we can express its area as a function of .   Why is washer area rather than ?   A region with a hole after rotation has washers, not disks. In your own words, why do we subtract two disk areas, and why is the radius difference the wrong quantity to square?    The hole is itself a disk. Area of a ring is outer disk minus inner disk. Squaring the thickness of the ring would describe a much smaller disk.     square cross sections   A solid has base in the -plane bounded by   Cross sections perpendicular to the -axis are squares.  At position , the side length of the square is the vertical distance from to :   So the cross-sectional area is   The volume is   Notice that no appears. These slices are squares, not disks.     semicircular cross sections   Use the same base region, but now let the cross sections perpendicular to the -axis be semicircles.  The diameter of the semicircle is the vertical distance   So the radius is   The area of a semicircle is   Thus   The volume is   The base region is the same as in the square example. The volume changed because the cross-sectional shape changed.   What matters in slicing  To compute volume by slicing, we need two pieces of information:   and   The base alone is not enough.       Choosing the slicing direction  The best slicing direction is the one that makes the cross-sectional area easy to write.  Sometimes -slices are best. Sometimes -slices are better.   How do you choose the slicing direction?   Sometimes -slices are best and sometimes -slices are better. In your own words, why is the best direction the one that makes the cross-sectional area easy to write, even though the volume being measured has not changed?    The notation or changes. The idea does not: add the area of one slice times its thickness.     rotating around the -axis   Rotate the region bounded by   around the -axis.  The region is easier to describe using horizontal slices. Solve   for :   For , a horizontal slice runs from the -axis to   When rotated around the -axis, it forms a disk of radius   So the cross-sectional area is   The volume is   The variable is , not , because the slices are perpendicular to the -axis.   Rotating around the -axis suggests horizontal slices and an integral in .          A slicing checklist  Volumes by slicing become manageable when the setup is organized.   Setting up a volume by slicing    Choose the slicing direction.    Identify the interval for the slicing variable.    Draw or describe a typical cross section.    Find its area or .    Integrate the cross-sectional area over the slicing interval.     For -slices,   For -slices,   The notation changes, but the idea does not.     Where this points  Disks and washers come from slices perpendicular to the axis of rotation. They are excellent when the cross sections are easy.  But some rotations make washers awkward. A vertical strip rotated around a vertical axis does not make a washer. It makes a thin cylindrical shell.  That gives a different small piece:   The next section builds that shell method from the same slicing idea.   "
