  "id": "sec-11-3-trigonometric-substitution",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-11-3-trigonometric-substitution.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "11.3",
  "title": "Trigonometric substitution",
  "body": " Trigonometric substitution  Trigonometric integrals used identities after trigonometric functions were already present. Trigonometric substitution uses those identities earlier. It creates trigonometric functions on purpose.  The reason is square roots.  Integrals such as   do not look trigonometric. But the expressions under the square roots resemble the three Pythagorean identities:     A trigonometric substitution chooses so that the square root becomes a simpler trigonometric expression. The square root does not vanish by magic. It vanishes because a right triangle or a circle was hidden inside it.   Square roots from circles and triangles  Start with   This expression comes from the circle   Solving for the upper half gives   A point on the circle of radius can be described by   Then   So   If we choose   then , and therefore   That is the whole idea. We choose the range of so that the square root is nonnegative without needing an absolute value.   Seed example  Find   Use   Then   and   The integral becomes   So   Use the half-angle identity   Then   Thus   Now return to . Since   we have   Also,   From the substitution,   and   Therefore   Substitute back:   So   The answer is more complicated than the integrand. That is normal. Antiderivatives often remember the substitution that created them.    Why does a square root such as hide a triangle?   The expression is the upper half of a circle. In your own words, why does choosing make the square root become , and why is that not magic but a Pythagorean identity?    The substitution is chosen so that becomes a perfect square. A point on the circle of radius already has coordinates that satisfy that identity.      , , and  The three common square-root patterns match the three Pythagorean identities.     Expression  Substitution  Identity  Resulting square root                         The table assumes the usual ranges of are chosen so that the square roots are nonnegative. For example, with   we usually choose   so that .  The substitutions also change the small step :     This is the same substitution rule as before. The integrand changes because the measuring step changes.   The three substitutions are right-triangle substitutions. The triangle helps us return to .               How do you match a square root to a substitution?   The three patterns , , and match sine, tangent, and secant substitutions. In your own words, why is the right substitution the one whose Pythagorean identity cancels the square root, rather than a trigonometric function chosen for familiarity?    Look at the sign pattern under the radical. Each identity is built to turn one of those three combinations into a square.     the pattern   Find   The expression under the square root has the form   with . Use   Then   and   where on the chosen interval.  So   From Section ,   Now return to . Since   the triangle gives   Therefore   The factor inside the logarithm changes the answer only by a constant, so it can be absorbed into . Thus      the pattern   Find   The expression under the square root has the form   with . Use   Then   and   The integral becomes   Cancel:   Use   Then   So   Now return to . Since   we have   Thus   The triangle also gives   Therefore   Hence   The condition is not a small detail. The substitution with covers . On another interval, such as , we would choose a different range or adjust the substitution. Antiderivatives are always local to an interval where the integrand is defined.      Returning to the original variable  An indefinite integral should end in the original variable unless the new variable has been clearly defined.  There are two common ways to return.   Two ways to return from     Method  What it does    Inverse trigonometric function  Uses equations such as or .    Right triangle  Uses the substitution to draw a triangle and read , , , or .      In many examples, both methods are needed. The angle itself becomes an inverse trigonometric function. Other expressions, such as or , are easiest to recover from the triangle.   Definite integrals: change the limits  For definite integrals, there is often no need to return to . We can change the limits instead.  Evaluate   Use   Then   and   on the chosen range.  Now change the limits.  When   we have   so   When   we have   so   Thus   So   No -triangle is needed at the end because the whole calculation stayed in the new variable after the limits changed.   A common mistake  Do not mix variables in a definite integral. After substituting , either change the limits to -limits or return the antiderivative to before evaluating.  The expression   after an -substitution is meaningless unless and have been converted into the corresponding -values.   The differential keeps the direction of the substitution. If increasing the new variable moves in the opposite direction, the sign of records that reversal. This is the same orientation idea we saw in definite integrals.    Why can a definite trigonometric substitution skip returning to ?   For a definite integral, we can change the limits to -values and never go back. In your own words, why are the limits part of the substitution, and when would you still need a triangle to return to the original variable?    An indefinite answer must be written in . A definite integral only needs the accumulated amount between the new endpoints.      Hyperbolic substitutions  This subsection is optional on a first reading.  Trigonometric substitutions rely on   and   There is another pair of functions built for expressions of the form and . The hyperbolic sine and cosine are defined by   They satisfy   Their derivatives are   Because   the substitution   turns   into   Because   the substitution   turns   into   when .   Why does the range of matter?   After substitution, a square root becomes something like . In your own words, why do we choose a range of so that the square root is nonnegative without an absolute value, and why can the same-looking formula fail on a different interval of ?    A triangle carries sign information. The substitution with covers , not the separate piece .     Example   For , find   Use   Then   and   So   Since   we have   Thus   Using   this can also be written as   The hyperbolic substitution gives the logarithmic answer more directly. It is not a new kind of calculus. It is another choice of variable matched to the shape of the square root.  Trigonometric substitution handles square roots by changing the geometry of the variable. The next family of integrals has no square roots at all. It is algebraic: one polynomial divided by another. There the right move is not to create a triangle, but to split one complicated fraction into simpler fractions.     "
