  "number": "13.6",
  "title": "First-order linear equations",
  "body": " First-order linear equations  Separable equations can be solved by pulling all ’s to one side and all ’s to the other. Many useful equations do not separate.  A tank may have salt entering and salt leaving. A bank account may earn interest while money is also being deposited. A cooling object may sit in a room whose temperature is changing.  These models have a common form:   That is the shape of a first-order linear equation.   Standard form  A first-order differential equation is linear if the unknown function and its derivative appear only to the first power and are not multiplied together.  The standard form is   Here   are known functions of . The unknown is .   First-order linear equation  A first-order linear differential equation has the form   If , the equation is called homogeneous . If , it is called nonhomogeneous .   The word linear means linear in and . The coefficient functions and may be complicated functions of .   is linear.   is linear.  But   is not linear, and   is not linear, because the unknown function appears nonlinearly.   A first linear example  Consider   This says that is being pushed upward by a constant input , while the term pulls against the current amount.  We can rewrite it as   The equilibrium occurs when   Thus   If , then , so the solution rises.  If , then , so the solution falls.  So we expect solutions to approach .  This particular equation can be solved by shifting the variable:   Then   and   So   Therefore   Since   we get   If   then   so   The solution is   This example was friendly because the right side was constant. We need a method that also works when and vary with time.    What makes a first-order equation linear?   The standard form is . In your own words, what does the word linear refer to here, and why can and still be complicated functions of without destroying linearity?    Linear means linear in the unknown and its derivative . The coefficients are allowed to depend on the independent variable.      Integrating factors  The product rule says   A linear equation has the left side   If we multiply the equation by a function , we get   We want the left side to be   For that to happen, we need   That is a separable equation for :   Integrate:   So we may choose   This function is called an integrating factor.   Integrating factor method  To solve   choose   Then   so multiplying the equation by gives   Integrate both sides and solve for .   The method is not a trick. It is the product rule used backward.   Example   Solve   Here   An integrating factor is   Multiply the equation by :   The left side is   So   Integrate:   Therefore   Use the initial condition:   So   Thus      Check  Differentiate:   Then   The exponential decay terms cancel:   The terms add:   So   Also,   The solution checks.    Why does an integrating factor turn the left side into a product rule?   Multiplying by converts into . In your own words, why is that not a trick, and what single sentence explains why the method works?    The factor is chosen so that . Then is exactly the derivative of the product .      Mixing and finance examples  Linear equations appear whenever there is a current amount and an outside input.   What story does the nonhomogeneous term tell?   Mixing tanks and continuous deposits produce linear equations with an outside input. In your own words, why does the solution combine an initial amount with accumulated weighted input, and what would mean in those stories?    After the product rule is restored, integration accumulates the forced term. A zero input is the homogeneous case: the amount then only decays or grows from its starting value.     salt entering and leaving a tank   A tank contains liters of water. Brine flows in at liters per minute with concentration   The well-mixed solution flows out at liters per minute, so the volume remains liters.  Let   Salt enters at the rate   gram per minute.  The concentration in the tank is   grams per liter, so salt leaves at the rate   grams per minute.  Thus   In linear standard form,   Use an integrating factor:   Multiply by :   The left side is   So   Integrate:   Therefore   If the tank initially contains pure water, then   So   and   Thus   The amount of salt approaches   That makes sense. The incoming concentration is grams per liter, and the tank holds liters. If the tank eventually matches the incoming concentration, it should contain   grams of salt.   With a constant incoming concentration, the salt amount approaches an equilibrium.         continuous deposits with interest   A bank account earns continuous interest at annual rate   Money is deposited continuously at   Let   Interest contributes   dollars per year. Deposits contribute   dollars per year. Therefore   Write this in standard linear form:   Here   An integrating factor is   Multiply:   The left side is   So   Integrate:   Since   we get   Thus   If the account starts empty,   then   so   Therefore   The formula has a natural interpretation. The deposits alone would add . The interest makes earlier deposits grow, so the balance is larger than for .  After years,   Since   we get   The account has about   after years.      Why the integrating factor works  The integrating factor method is worth seeing in one clean line.  Start with   Suppose we can find a positive function such that   Then multiplying the equation by gives   But   so the left side becomes   By the product rule,   Therefore   Now integrate.  The whole method rests on choosing so that the left side becomes one derivative.  Since   we may take   The exponential is useful because its derivative brings down the derivative of the exponent:   So    The product-rule sentence  The integrating factor is chosen so that   becomes   That is why the method works.   There is also a definite-integral version that is useful for initial value problems.  Suppose   and are continuous on an interval containing . Define   and   Then   Multiplying the equation by gives   Integrate from to :   Since and ,   Therefore   This formula is not always the easiest way to compute, but it tells the whole structure:    Why the interval matters  The formula assumes that and are continuous on the interval where we solve the equation. If a coefficient breaks, the solution may have to be handled on separate intervals.  Consider   This is linear, but the coefficient   is not defined at   So we solve on intervals that avoid , such as   or   On , an integrating factor is   Then   so   Integrate:   Thus   The formula cannot be continued through because of the term , and the original equation itself is not defined at .   Coefficient warning  A solution of a differential equation lives on an interval where the equation makes sense. Singular coefficients split the real line into separate problems.   First-order equations already show several personalities. Some are separable. Some are linear. Some are best understood by a phase line. Some require numerical methods.  But many laws of motion do not stop at the first derivative. A spring, a pendulum in a small-angle approximation, and a vibrating string involve acceleration. Their laws begin with   That is the next door: differential equations where the unknown function is governed by its second derivative.    Why can a singular coefficient split one equation into separate problems?   The equation is linear, but is undefined at . In your own words, why must a solution live on an interval where the equation makes sense, and why can a formula such as not be continued through zero?    Continuous coefficients are part of the hypotheses. A singularity of or is a wall between intervals, not a point to be crossed casually.     "
