  "title": "Why can a singular coefficient split one equation into separate problems?",
  "body": " Why can a singular coefficient split one equation into separate problems?   The equation is linear, but is undefined at . In your own words, why must a solution live on an interval where the equation makes sense, and why can a formula such as not be continued through zero?    Continuous coefficients are part of the hypotheses. A singularity of or is a wall between intervals, not a point to be crossed casually.   "
