  "number": "14.2",
  "title": "Calculus with parametric curves",
  "body": " Calculus with parametric curves  A parametric curve gives two functions:   The graph is drawn in the -plane, but the motion is controlled by .  So a slope question must be translated.   The parameter tells us two easier facts:   and   The slope is the ratio of those two rates.   Slope from and  Return to the sideways parabola   At , the point is   The coordinate rates are   At ,   This means that near , a small increase in time produces about twice as much horizontal change as vertical change. The tangent slope should be   That is exactly the ratio    Parametric slope formula  Suppose and are differentiable near , and   Then the slope of the parametric curve at is   evaluated at .    Why the formula is true  If can be used as the input near , then changes with through . The chain rule says   If   we can divide:   The condition matters. If is not changing at that instant, then a slope measured as vertical change divided by horizontal change may be infinite or may require more analysis.    Why is parametric slope the ratio of two time rates?   A graph asks how fast changes with respect to , but a parametric curve gives rates with respect to . In your own words, why is the natural translation, and why must for that quotient to make sense?    Slope is vertical change over horizontal change. If is not changing at that instant, the curve is not locally a graph over the -axis.     slope on a sideways parabola   For   we have   At , the slope is   At , the slope is   At , the formula is not allowed because   The point at is   the vertex of the sideways parabola. The tangent there is vertical.   For , the slope is when .      The parametric formula has recovered exactly what the geometry suggests.     slope on the circle   For   we have   Therefore   as long as .  At , the point is   and the slope is   The tangent line slopes downward at , as expected on the upper right part of the circle.      Horizontal and vertical tangents  The formula   makes horizontal and vertical tangents easy to identify.  A horizontal tangent has slope . This happens when   while   A vertical tangent has infinite slope. This happens when   while    Horizontal and vertical tangents for parametric curves  For a differentiable parametrization , :    If both derivatives are , the test does not decide the tangent. More work is needed.    tangents on the unit circle   For   we have   Horizontal tangents occur when   so   At these times,   The points are   Vertical tangents occur when   so   At these times,   The corresponding points are    On the unit circle, gives horizontal tangents and gives vertical tangents.      This is a good example of the parametric method. The circle is not a single graph , but the tangent slopes are still easy to find.     A warning when both derivatives vanish  The conditions above deliberately exclude the case   At such a time, the moving point has zero speed. The curve may still have a tangent, but the simple quotient gives   which tells us nothing.  For example, let   At ,   The slope formula is not legal at . For ,   As , this slope approaches . The curve has a horizontal tangent at the cusp, but we had to find it by a limit, not by substituting into the formula.   Moral  When both and are zero, do not use the horizontal or vertical tangent test blindly. Study the nearby motion.   We will meet this warning again when a curve stops, reverses, or forms a cusp.    How do you recognize horizontal and vertical tangents parametrically?   Horizontal tangents occur when is zero while is not; vertical tangents reverse those roles. In your own words, why do those tests match the geometry of a moving point, and why is a circle a good example of needing the parametric language?    If the point moves only horizontally, the tangent is horizontal. A circle is not a single graph , but its coordinate rates still determine the tangent.      Concavity  Concavity asks how the slope changes as changes.  For an ordinary function , we write   for the derivative of with respect to .  For a parametric curve, is first a function of . To differentiate it with respect to , use the same idea as before:   provided .  Thus   This formula says:   It is the same slope idea one level higher.   What should you do when both coordinate derivatives vanish?   If , the slope formula gives . In your own words, why does that not automatically mean the curve has no tangent, and what extra look nearby is required?    Zero speed is a pause of the parametrization. The geometric tangent, if it exists, must be read from nearby slopes or by eliminating the parameter.     concavity of the sideways parabola   For   we found   Differentiate with respect to :   Since   we get   For , this is negative. The upper branch is concave down.  For , this is positive. The lower branch is concave up.  That matches the picture of   The upper branch can be written as   which bends downward. The lower branch can be written as   which bends upward.     concavity of the upper semicircle   Use   This traces the upper semicircle. We already found   Differentiate with respect to :   Also,   Therefore   On ,   so   The upper semicircle is concave down everywhere. This agrees with the graph    Concavity for parametric curves  To find concavity:   The result is , whenever the divisions make sense.   The phrase “whenever the divisions make sense” is not a minor detail. Parametric curves can have vertical tangents, cusps, and pauses. At such points, the algebra may need a separate geometric check.      Motion in the plane as a bridge to vectors  Parametric equations are not only a way to draw curves. They are also the natural language of plane motion.  If a particle has position   then its horizontal and vertical velocities are   We can package them as the pair   This pair points in the direction of motion. Its length is the speed:   The acceleration components are   so the acceleration pair is   This is the beginning of vector calculus, but the idea is already familiar: each coordinate is an ordinary single-variable function of time.   How does plane motion package calculus into pairs?   Position has velocity components and . In your own words, why is that pair already a bridge to vectors, and what does its length measure?    Each coordinate is an ordinary function of time. The pair points in the direction of motion; its length is the speed.     projectile motion   Suppose a ball is launched from height feet with horizontal velocity ft\/s and initial vertical velocity ft\/s. Ignoring air resistance, its position can be modeled by    where is measured in seconds.  The coordinate velocities are   The horizontal velocity is constant. The vertical velocity decreases because gravity pulls downward.  The acceleration components are   So the acceleration is   which points straight downward.  The slope of the trajectory is   At , the slope is   At the top of the flight, the vertical velocity is :   so   At that time,   so the tangent is horizontal.  We can also eliminate . Since   we have   Substitute into :   Simplify:   The path is a parabola. The parametrization tells how the ball moves along it.   The equations give the path and the motion along it.      This example brings several ideas together.  The Cartesian equation   shows the shape of the path.  The parametric equations   show the motion: where the ball is at each time, how fast it moves horizontally and vertically, when it reaches the top, and how gravity changes the vertical velocity.      What comes next  The derivative questions have now been answered for parametric curves.   gives tangent slopes, when .   gives concavity, when the divisions are valid.  But a moving point also travels a distance. Earlier, distance came from accumulated speed. The same idea now applies in the plane.  If   then the length of a parametric curve should be an integral of this speed.  That is the next question:    "
