  "number": "16.3",
  "title": "Representing functions by power series",
  "body": " Representing functions by power series  The last section gave us permission to work with power series inside their radius of convergence. We can add, multiply, differentiate, and integrate them term by term.  Now we use that permission in the opposite direction.  Instead of starting with a power series and asking where it converges, we start with a function and ask:   The first seed is the geometric series. From that one seed, differentiation, integration, and substitution will grow new series.   Geometric series as the seed  Start with the finite sum   Multiply by :   Everything cancels except the first and last terms:   So, if ,   Now let . If   then   Therefore   Thus   This is our first power series representation of a function.   Partial sums of the geometric series approach on .      The equality   is not true for every . At , the right side is   which diverges. At , the right side is   which also diverges.  So the formula always carries its home with it:   A power series representation is not only an identity. It is an identity together with an interval where the identity is valid.   Why is a power series representation an identity together with an interval?   The geometric series gives only for . In your own words, why is the interval part of the mathematics rather than a footnote, and why is the formula false at even though the left side is defined there?    The right side is a limit of partial sums. Outside those partial sums do not approach the function, so the two sides are not the same object.      Series for  The geometric series is a machine. Once we know   we can replace by other expressions, as long as the new expression has absolute value less than .   replacing by   Since   put   Then   So   This will become the seed for the logarithm.     replacing by   Put   Then   The condition is   which is the same as   Thus      a different radius   Find a power series for   centered at .  Rewrite the function so that the geometric form appears:   Now use the geometric series with   Then   when   So   The radius is , not , because the actual ratio is .     a series centered at   Find a power series for   centered at .  Write as   Then   Use the series   with   Thus   or, more compactly,   The center is . The series converges when is within distance of that center:   This makes sense. The function has trouble at , and that point is exactly distance from the center .     Differentiating the geometric series  Inside ,   Differentiate both sides:   The left side is   The right side is   Therefore   This is the same product series we saw earlier:   The algebra and the calculus agree.   A useful workflow  To represent a function by a power series:    Start from a known series, usually the geometric series.    Rewrite the function until the known pattern appears.    Substitute carefully.    State the convergence condition.    If you differentiate or integrate, keep the same radius but test endpoints again.     The endpoint warning is not decoration. The next examples show why.    How can one seed series grow new representations?   Once the geometric series is known, substitution, differentiation, and integration produce series for other functions. In your own words, why must a substitution keep the new inner expression inside the original radius, and why do differentiated or integrated series keep the radius but not the endpoints?    Replacing by is legal only where . Calculus operations are justified inside the radius; the endpoints are a separate numerical question after the operation.      Series for  The logarithm is connected to   because   We already found   Integrate both sides from to . On the left,   On the right,   Therefore   The theorem on integrating power series gives the equality inside the radius. The endpoints must be tested separately.  At , the series becomes   This converges by the Alternating Series Test.  At , the series becomes   which diverges. This also matches the function side, because is not defined at .  So the power series has interval of convergence   There is one subtle point: term-by-term integration directly proved the formula only for . Why is the endpoint allowed in the identity?  Here is a concrete remainder argument.  For every ,   Integrate from to :   The remainder satisfies   Since   the remainder goes to . Therefore   The endpoint did not come for free. It came from a separate estimate.   Why can integration pick up an endpoint that differentiation would lose?   Integrating the series for produces the series for on . In your own words, why is the identity first proved only inside the radius, and why is the endpoint a separate argument rather than an automatic gift?    Term-by-term integration is guaranteed in the open interval. At the series becomes the alternating harmonic series, whose convergence and identification with need their own reasons.      Series for  The inverse tangent is connected to   because   Start from   Put   Then   Integrate from to :   The left side is   The right side is   Thus   Now test the endpoints.  At , the series becomes   which converges by the Alternating Series Test.  At , it becomes   which also converges.  So the interval of convergence is   Again, equality at the endpoints needs justification. The finite identity   gives, after integration from to ,   The remainder has absolute value at most   which tends to . Therefore   This is one of the famous surprises of power series: a series built from odd reciprocals knows the number .    Estimating from a series  The identity   gives a way to estimate . The Alternating Series Estimation Theorem says that the error after stopping is at most the size of the first omitted term.  If we stop after   then   Multiplying by , the error in is at most   This is useful, but slow. To guarantee an error less than , we need   so   That means about terms.  The series is beautiful, but it is not efficient.  A better idea is to use the same arctangent series at smaller inputs. Smaller powers shrink faster.  Let   Then   Since and are positive acute angles,   Thus   Now use the arctangent series:   With and , the terms shrink quickly:   Using the first five terms from each arctangent series gives   The alternating error estimate gives an error less than   The same power series produced both estimates. The difference was the input. At , powers shrink slowly. At and , powers shrink quickly.   Computational lesson  A series representation is not only a formula. It is also an algorithm. Its usefulness depends on how fast its terms shrink.   The geometric series gave us logarithms and inverse tangents. But the functions   do not obviously come from the geometric series. To reach them, we need a new question:   That question leads to Taylor polynomials.   Why can a slowly convergent series still be the right idea?   The Leibniz series for is beautiful but slow, while arctangent at smaller inputs is faster. In your own words, why does a series representation become a computational tool, and why does using the same series closer to its center change the efficiency?    A series is useful only where its remainder is controllable. Smaller inputs make powers shrink faster, so the same identity can be a poor calculator or a good one depending on where it is evaluated.     "
