  "number": "16.4",
  "title": "Taylor polynomials",
  "body": " Taylor polynomials  Power series gave us infinite polynomials. But before an infinite polynomial can be trusted, we should understand the finite ones that approximate a function near one point.  The derivative already gave us the first approximation:   That is the tangent line. It matches the value and slope of at .  Taylor’s idea is to continue the matching.   The result is a polynomial built from local information.   Matching value and derivatives  Begin with the function   near .  We know   So the constant approximation should be   We also know   The linear approximation should match value and slope:   Now ask for a quadratic approximation   The coefficient should be chosen so that the second derivative also matches. Since   we want   But   so   Thus   For a cubic approximation,   Since   and   we need   So   The factorials are not mysterious. They come from differentiating powers:    Taylor polynomials for match more derivatives at the center .      The polynomials   are not random. Each one matches more local information about at .     polynomial  what it matches at     value     value and first derivative     value, first derivative, and second derivative     value through third derivative     This is the pattern Taylor polynomials formalize.   Why does a Taylor polynomial match derivatives at a center?   The tangent line matches value and slope. A Taylor polynomial continues the matching through higher derivatives. In your own words, why is that the right way to build a local polynomial, and what local information is each extra coefficient recording?    Near a point, the first things a smooth function reveals are its value, its rate of change, its bending, and so on. Matching those derivatives makes the polynomial share the function’s local shape.      Linear, quadratic, and cubic approximations  The first Taylor polynomial is the tangent line:   It matches and at .  The second Taylor polynomial adds curvature information:   The third Taylor polynomial adds the third derivative:   The numbers and are    approximating a square root   Find the cubic Taylor polynomial for   centered at , and use it to approximate   First compute derivatives:      At ,   Therefore   So   To approximate , use   So . Then   Compute:   Thus   The approximation is good because is close to the center . The polynomial was built for nearby inputs.     What each term is doing  The approximation   uses the tangent line. It says that near ,   initially rises at rate .  The term   corrects for concavity. The square-root graph bends downward, so the tangent line slightly overestimates. The negative quadratic term pulls the approximation down.  The term   makes a smaller correction to the correction.  A Taylor polynomial is not just a formula. Each coefficient records derivative information at the center.    What is each Taylor term doing?   For near , the linear term follows the tangent, the quadratic term corrects for concavity, and the cubic term corrects the correction. In your own words, why is a Taylor polynomial not just a formula, and why do later terms matter less when you stay close to the center?    Each coefficient is derivative data at one point. Close to the center, higher powers of are small, so the first few matched derivatives dominate the picture.      Taylor polynomial centered at  Now write the general formula.  Suppose has derivatives up to order at . The degree Taylor polynomial for centered at is   In sigma notation,   Here    Why the coefficients have this form  Suppose   Evaluate at . Every term with a factor disappears, so   Differentiate once:   At ,   Differentiate twice:   Differentiate times. At , the only surviving contribution to the -th derivative comes from   It gives   To make , we must choose   That is exactly the Taylor coefficient.    Why do the Taylor coefficients involve factorials?   The degree Taylor polynomial centered at has coefficients . In your own words, why does differentiating a power a total of times produce a factorial, and why is that factorial needed to match ?    Differentiating down to a constant multiplies by . To make the constant equal , the coefficient must already contain the reciprocal factorial.     centered at   The logarithm is not defined at , so a polynomial centered at would be a bad idea. But behaves nicely near .  Let   Then      The cubic Taylor polynomial centered at is   So   To approximate , use . Then , and   So   This matches the earlier logarithm series in another language. If we write   then   and the polynomial becomes   The center changed the notation, but not the idea.      Maclaurin polynomials  A Taylor polynomial centered at has a special name.   Maclaurin polynomial  The degree Taylor polynomial centered at is called the degree  Maclaurin polynomial :    Many of the most familiar series begin as Maclaurin polynomials.     All derivatives of are , and   So every derivative at equals . Therefore   for .       The derivatives of cycle:   At ,   So the derivatives at follow the pattern   Thus the degree Maclaurin polynomial is        For , the derivative values at are   So the degree Maclaurin polynomial is      function  early Maclaurin polynomials                   These are still finite polynomials. We have not yet proved that the corresponding infinite series equal the functions. That distinction matters.     A necessary warning  Taylor polynomials require derivatives at the center.  The function   is continuous at , but it is not differentiable at . The left slope is , and the right slope is . There is no single number .  So there is no first-degree Taylor polynomial for centered at that matches the derivative. The needed local data does not exist.   Moral  A Taylor polynomial is built from derivatives at a center. If those derivatives do not exist, the polynomial cannot be built in the usual way.   There is another warning. Even when the polynomial exists, it is only a local approximation until we understand its error.  For , the polynomial   is good near . It is not automatically good far away. Taylor polynomials are promises about contact at a point, not yet promises about accuracy on a whole interval.  That is the next question:   The answer is the remainder term. Without it, approximation has no scale.    Why is a Taylor polynomial a local promise?   Taylor polynomials require derivatives at the center, and even when they exist they are not automatically accurate far away. In your own words, why can have no first-degree Taylor polynomial at , and why is matching at a point not yet a statement about error on an interval?    Without a derivative, there is no slope to match. With a derivative, contact at a point still says nothing about how large the leftover gap becomes as you move away. That gap is the remainder.     "
