  "title": "Taylor series for elementary functions",
  "body": " Taylor series for elementary functions  Taylor’s theorem says   If the remainders satisfy   as , then the Taylor polynomials approach the function itself:   That limit is an infinite series.   Taylor series  If has derivatives of all orders at , its Taylor series centered at is   When , this is called the Maclaurin series:    There is a warning hidden in the definition. The Taylor series is built from the derivatives of at one point. It may converge only for some -values. Even when it converges, it must still be checked that it converges to .   For the elementary functions in this section, we will prove or recall why the equality is valid.    The exponential function is the cleanest Taylor series because every derivative is the same:   At ,   So   for every . The Maclaurin series is therefore   or   Now we justify the equality.  The degree Maclaurin polynomial is   Taylor’s theorem gives   for some between and . For a fixed , the value is bounded. For example,   for between and . Therefore   The factor   tends to , because factorials eventually grow faster than any fixed power. Thus   for every real .  So the exponential series is valid for all real :    When does a Taylor series equal the function?   If has derivatives of all orders at , it has a Taylor series. In your own words, why is writing that series not the same as proving equals the series, and why is the missing bridge?    The series is built from derivative data at one point. The function equals the limit of its Taylor polynomials only where the leftovers vanish.     estimating   Use terms through :   Compute:   The remainder after degree is   for some between and . Since on this interval,   Now   so   Thus   with error less than .      and  The derivatives of cycle:   At ,   So the derivative values are   Only odd powers appear:   or   For , the derivative values at are   Only even powers appear:   or   The equality is valid for all real . Here is why. Every derivative of or is one of   All of these have absolute value at most . Taylor’s theorem gives   For each fixed , this tends to . Therefore the Taylor series for sine and cosine converge to the functions for every real .     Taylor polynomials for are best near the center , then improve outward as more terms are added.       Why do some elementary series live on the whole line and others do not?   The series for , , and are valid for every real , while and are not. In your own words, why is that not a defect of the functions, and what is the series actually reporting?    Factorials in the denominator overpower any fixed power of . Geometric-type series are limited by a radius coming from a nearby singularity or from the original geometric seed, even if the function itself is defined more widely.     estimating   Use   At ,   Because the sine series is alternating with decreasing term sizes for this input, the error is at most the first omitted term:   Since   we get   Thus   with error less than .       The logarithm series was already discovered by integrating the geometric series:   Integrating from to gave   Now we see why these are the Taylor coefficients.  Let   Then   Also,     The pattern is   At ,   Therefore the coefficient of is   So   or   The interval of convergence is   At , this gives the important identity   At , the series becomes   which diverges. This matches the function side: is not defined at .   estimating   Here   so . Use four terms:   Compute:   The first omitted term has size   Therefore   with error less than .  The logarithm series is most useful when is close to . That means the number inside the logarithm,   is close to .       The inverse tangent also came from the geometric series. Since   and   integrating from to gave   Thus   The interval of convergence is   At ,   so   This identity is beautiful, but slow. The terms shrink like odd reciprocals. As we saw earlier, using smaller inputs such as and makes the same series much faster.   estimating   Use   At ,   The first omitted term has size   Therefore   with error less than .      Binomial series  The geometric series is one member of a larger family.  For a positive integer , the binomial theorem says   For example,   But calculus allows the exponent to be any real number , not only a nonnegative integer. The price is that the polynomial usually becomes an infinite series.  First define the generalized binomial coefficients:   and, for ,   When is a nonnegative integer, this agrees with the usual binomial coefficient, and the coefficients eventually become zero.   Binomial series  For any real number ,   for   If is a nonnegative integer, the series terminates and becomes the ordinary binomial theorem.   Written out, the series is    Why this is a Taylor series  Let   Then    and in general,   At ,   So the Taylor coefficient is   That gives the formal Taylor series.    Why it converges to for  Let   The Ratio Test gives radius of convergence , unless the series terminates. Inside , power series can be differentiated term by term.  Differentiate and multiply by . A coefficient check gives   Also,   The function   satisfies the same equation:   and   Now compare them by forming   On ,   So is constant. Since   we get   Therefore   for .    the square-root series   Take   Then   So   This explains the coefficients we found earlier from Taylor polynomials.     estimating   Here   Use the terms through :   Compute:   The next term has size   So   with error less than .     Recovering the geometric series  Take   Then   This is the familiar series   Replacing by gives   So the geometric series is a binomial series with exponent .    Why is the binomial series a Taylor series?   The binomial series for comes from matching derivatives at , and it recovers the geometric series when . In your own words, why does that make the geometric series part of the Taylor library rather than a rival construction, and why must still be respected?    The coefficients are exactly the Taylor coefficients of . The radius is inherited from the same geometric limitation that already governed .      A table of common Taylor series  The most common Maclaurin series are worth keeping together.     function  Maclaurin series  where valid                                        For the binomial series, endpoints must be tested separately unless the series terminates.   How to use the table  A Taylor series is not only a memorized formula. Each formula has three parts:   Leaving out the interval is leaving out part of the mathematics.   We now have a library of infinite polynomial representations. The next section turns that library into a tool for computation: estimating function values, evaluating limits, approximating integrals, and even solving differential equations by matching coefficients.   Why does a Taylor formula have three parts?   Each common series in the table is a function, a series, and an interval where the identity is valid. In your own words, why is leaving out the interval leaving out part of the mathematics?    An equality of functions is a statement about a domain. Outside that domain the series may diverge, or it may converge to something else.     "
