  "title": "Factoring and completing the square",
  "body": " Factoring and completing the square  Factoring turns addition into multiplication. That is why it is so useful in calculus. Multiplication can cancel. Multiplication has signs we can track. Multiplication reveals zeros.   Common factors  The first question in factoring is always:   For example,   The common factor was present in both terms.   Example   Factor   Each term contains , so   The quadratic does not factor over the real numbers, because its discriminant is   So the real factorization is       Special products  Some patterns appear often enough that they should become automatic.     name  identity    Difference of squares     Perfect square     Perfect square     Difference of cubes     Sum of cubes       Example   Factor   This is a difference of squares:      Example   Factor   This is a perfect square:   Therefore       Factoring quadratics  A quadratic has the form   When , factoring asks for two numbers whose product is and whose sum is .   Example   Factor   We need two numbers with product and sum . They are and . Thus      Example   Factor   We need two numbers with product and sum . They are and . Thus   When the leading coefficient is not , one reliable method is grouping.     Example   Factor   First multiply the leading coefficient and constant term:   We need two numbers with product and sum . They are and . Rewrite the middle term:   Group:   Now factor out the common binomial:       Why factoring matters in limits  A common calculus move is to simplify a difference quotient.   Example   Simplify   Expand:   Factor:   Therefore   Since ,   The cancellation is legal because . That condition matters. The original fraction is undefined at , but the simplified expression shows what the fraction approaches as gets close to .      Completing the square  Factoring is not always possible. Completing the square is often the next best move.  The pattern is   So    Example   Complete the square:   Half of is . Square it:   Add and subtract :   Therefore   This form immediately tells us that   for every real , because .   Completing the square reveals the vertex and the minimum value.         Example   Complete the square for   First factor out the leading coefficient from the quadratic and linear terms:   Complete the square inside the parentheses. Half of is , and :   Thus   So   This form tells us that the minimum value is , reached at .      Quadratic formula  Completing the square also gives the quadratic formula. For   the solutions are   The expression   is called the discriminant . It tells how many real roots the quadratic has.     discriminant  real roots     two distinct real roots     one repeated real root     no real roots      Example   Solve   Here   So   Thus   Therefore      "
