  "number": "B.1",
  "title": "Lines",
  "body": " Lines  The first graph in calculus is often a line. Constant velocity gives a line. A tangent approximation gives a line. Newton’s method follows a line. The Mean Value Theorem compares a tangent slope with a secant slope.  So lines deserve to be completely familiar.   Slope  A nonvertical line has a constant steepness. That steepness is its slope .  If a line passes through two distinct points   with , then its slope is   In words,    Slope measures vertical change per horizontal change.       Example   Find the slope of the line through   The change in is   The change in is   Therefore   The line rises units for every units it moves to the right.     Example   Find the slope of the line through   We compute   The negative sign means the line falls as increases.   Vertical lines  A vertical line has no slope, because its run is . The slope formula would require division by .  A vertical line has equation   A horizontal line has slope and equation        Point-slope form  If a line has slope and passes through the point   then every point on the line satisfies   when . Multiplying by gives the most useful form:   This is called the point-slope form of a line.  It is the same form used for tangent lines in calculus:    Example   Find an equation of the line through   with slope   Use point-slope form:   Thus   If we solve for ,   so       Slope-intercept form  If a line has slope and crosses the -axis at   then point-slope form gives   So   This is the slope-intercept form . The number is the -intercept .   Example   The line   has slope   and -intercept   So it crosses the -axis at   It falls units for every unit it moves to the right.      Line through two points  To find a line through two points:    Example   Find an equation of the line through   First compute the slope:   Use point-slope form with :   This is already a good equation. If we want slope-intercept form,   so       General form  Every line can be written in the form   where , , and are constants, and and are not both .  If , then we can solve for :   so   Therefore the slope is   and the -intercept is   If , the equation becomes   so   That is a vertical line.   Example   Find the slope and intercepts of   Solve for :   Thus   So the slope is   and the -intercept is   To find the -intercept, set :   so   Thus the -intercept is    The intercepts are often the quickest way to sketch a line in general form.          Parallel and perpendicular lines  Slope detects parallel and perpendicular lines.   Parallel lines  Two nonvertical lines are parallel exactly when they have the same slope.    Perpendicular lines  Two nonvertical lines with slopes and are perpendicular exactly when   Equivalently,    A vertical line is perpendicular to a horizontal line.   Example   Find the line through   parallel to   First solve the given line for :   so   The slope is   A parallel line has the same slope. Using point-slope form,   Thus      Example   Find the line through   perpendicular to   The given line has slope   A perpendicular line has slope   because   Thus   So      "
