  "number": "B.3",
  "title": "Parabolas, ellipses, and hyperbolas",
  "body": " Parabolas, ellipses, and hyperbolas  Lines and circles are not the only curves that appear in calculus. Parabolas are the basic graphs of quadratic functions. Ellipses appear in planetary motion and optimization. Hyperbolas appear in reciprocal functions, asymptotes, and polar coordinates.  These curves are called conic sections , or simply conics , because they can be obtained by slicing a cone with a plane. For our purposes, the most important skill is algebraic recognition.    Parabolas  A vertical parabola has vertex form   Its vertex is   If , it opens upward. If , it opens downward.  A horizontal parabola has form   If , it opens to the right. If , it opens to the left.   Example   Describe the graph of   The vertex is   Since , the parabola opens upward. Compared with   it is shifted right , shifted down , and vertically stretched by a factor of .   Vertex form shows the shift and stretch immediately.         Example   Rewrite   in vertex form.  Complete the square:   Therefore   So   The vertex is       Focus and directrix form  A parabola can also be defined as a distance condition: points equidistant from a fixed point and a fixed line.  For a vertical parabola with vertex ,   has focus   and directrix   The number gives the signed distance from the vertex to the focus. If , the parabola opens upward. If , it opens downward.  For a horizontal parabola,   has focus   and directrix    Example   Find the focus and directrix of   Compare with   So   The vertex is the origin. Therefore the focus is   and the directrix is       Ellipses  An ellipse is an oval. Its standard equation is   The center is   The horizontal semi-axis length is . The vertical semi-axis length is . The full width is , and the full height is .   The ellipse has semi-axis lengths and .       Example   Describe the ellipse   The center is   The horizontal semi-axis length is   because . The vertical semi-axis length is   because .  So the ellipse has width and height .      Hyperbolas  A hyperbola has two branches and two asymptotes.  A horizontal hyperbola has standard form   It opens left and right. Its center is . Its vertices are   Its asymptotes are   A vertical hyperbola has standard form   It opens up and down. Its vertices are   Its asymptotes are    Example   Describe   This is a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin. Here   The vertices are   The asymptotes are    A hyperbola approaches its asymptotes but does not cross them far from the center.         completing the square for a hyperbola   Put   in standard form.  Group terms:   Complete the square inside each group:   and   Substitute:   Expand the constants:   So   Divide by :   This is a vertical hyperbola with center   Since and , the vertices are   Thus the vertices are   The asymptotes are       Recognizing conics quickly  When no -term appears, the squared terms give a quick first test.     equation pattern  shape  main clue    one squared variable  parabola  only or only    and , same sign  circle or ellipse  both squared terms add    and , opposite signs  hyperbola  squared terms subtract     A circle is the special ellipse in which the two squared terms have the same coefficient after completing the square.   Example   Identify the type of conic:   Both squared terms appear and have the same sign, so the graph is an ellipse or possibly a circle. Complete the square:   Now   and   So   Thus   So   Divide by :   This is an ellipse centered at      "
