  "title": "Addition formulas",
  "body": " Addition formulas  The basic identities came from one angle. The addition formulas tell how two angles combine.  They are the source of many identities used in differentiation and integration.   Cosine of a difference  Take two points on the unit circle:   The distance between these two points depends only on the angle between them, which is .  Using the distance formula,   Expand:   Group terms:   Since each point is on the unit circle,   Thus   On the other hand, the same distance is the distance from to the point at angle . That squared distance is   Expand:   Using   this becomes   The two squared distances are equal, so   Therefore   This identity is the anchor. The other addition formulas follow from it.    Sine and cosine addition formulas  The addition formulas are:      A common mistake is to distribute sine or cosine over addition. That is not correct:   and   For example,   but   So    Example   Find the exact value of   Notice that   Using the sine addition formula,   Substitute exact values:   Thus      Example   Simplify   Use   Then   Since   we get       Tangent addition formulas  Because   the tangent formulas follow from the sine and cosine formulas.   and   These formulas are valid where the expressions involved are defined.   Example   Find   Write   Then   Since   we have   Rationalize or simplify:   The numerator is   Thus   This negative answer makes sense because lies in Quadrant II, where tangent is negative.      Double-angle and half-angle identities  Set in the addition formulas.  For sine:   Thus   For cosine:   Thus   Using   we also get   and   Solving these last two formulas for and gives the power-reduction identities:   and   These identities are especially useful in integration.   Example   Rewrite   without a square.  Use   So   This form is easier to integrate than .      Product-to-sum identities  Products of sines and cosines appear in Fourier series and in trigonometric integrals. The product-to-sum identities convert products into sums.      Example   Rewrite   as a sum.  Use   With   we get   Thus      "
