  "title": "Trigonometric equations",
  "body": " Trigonometric equations  A trigonometric equation usually has many solutions because trigonometric functions repeat.  Solving one means doing two things:   and then    Solving by the unit circle  Start with a simple equation:   On one full cycle,   the unit circle shows two solutions:   Since sine has period , all solutions are   The integer means any whole number:    Example   Solve   on   Cosine is negative in Quadrants II and III. The reference angle is   Thus the solutions on one full cycle are   If the problem asks for all real solutions, then      Example   Solve   for all real .  Tangent has period . One solution is   Therefore all solutions are       Solving after using identities  Many trigonometric equations require an identity before the unit circle can finish the problem.   Example   Solve   on   Factor:   So   or   The first equation gives   The second equation gives   so   Therefore the solutions are   The interval does not include , so is not listed.     Example   Solve   on   First solve for the angle . Since runs from to , the angle runs from to .  Cosine equals at   on the first full cycle, and then repeats after . In the interval , we have   Divide by :       Avoiding lost or extra solutions  Trigonometric equations invite two common mistakes.  First, dividing by a trigonometric expression can lose solutions.  Second, squaring both sides can introduce extra solutions.   division can lose solutions   Solve   on   Do not divide by . Instead, move all terms to one side:   Factor:   So   or   On ,   at   Also,   at   So the solutions are   If we had divided by , we would have lost both cases where .     squaring can add solutions   Solve   on   A direct method is to move to tangent. If   then , because if , then , not . So we may divide by :   Thus   On , the solutions are   If instead we squared both sides, we would get   That equation has four solutions on :   But   is false at   Squaring erased signs. Always check solutions after squaring.     "
